 "note": "kernel-only refresh: same operators and sampling protocol as CB1; startup/example/ALARA legs superseded by P40 identical-data census or unchanged",
 "regression_attribution": {
  "finding": "ACTINV 1.1.2 is ~5x slower than CB1's 1.0.0 at 1024 states and now LOSES to OpenMC's Python CRAM48 at the largest size (ratio ~0.7x vs CB1's 2.83x)",
  "cause": "commit 35d5448 replaced raw LU solves with solve_refined (compensated-residual iterative refinement, up to 5 iterations per shifted solve) across all CRAM paths - a correctness fix for phantom radioactive parents on ill-conditioned activation matrices",
  "attribution_measurement": "with solve_refined temporarily reverted to f.solve, the 1024-state median dropped 42.5ms -> 8.4ms (5.0x) on the same binary/environment - refinement accounts for essentially the entire regression; reverted back, refinement is the shipped behavior",
  "no_refinement_reference_medians_ms": {
   "2": 0.0184,
   "32": 0.3787,
   "256": 2.541,
   "1024": 8.4059
  },
  "judgement": "the cost is the price of the trace-inventory correctness fix; it is reported, not hidden. A selective/adaptive refinement scheme could recover most of the speed on well-conditioned poles but is untested - left as open work",
  "environment_note": "both arms ran thread-pinned in a bounded cgroup (CPUQuota 200%); absolute times are noisier than CB1's uncapped host but the refined-vs-raw A/B attribution is unambiguous"
 }
